\begin{example}\label{BernoulliExample}
Let $X = (X_1, X_2, ..., X_5)$ be iid Bernoulli($\theta$) random variables with joint probability mass function $p_\theta(x) = \theta^{T(\mathbf{x})}(1-\theta)^{5-T(\mathbf{x})}$ where $T(x) = \sum_ix_i$, and consider testing null hypothesis $H_0:\theta = 0.5$ against alternative hypothesis $H_1:\theta = 0.8$.
\end{example}

expanded_sketch:
To construct the most powerful level $0.1$ test in
\begin{example}\label{BernoulliExample}
Let $X = (X_1, X_2, ..., X_5)$ be iid Bernoulli($\theta$) random variables with joint probability mass function $p_\theta(x) = \theta^{T(\mathbf{x})}(1-\theta)^{5-T(\mathbf{x})}$ where $T(x) = \sum_ix_i$, and consider testing null hypothesis $H_0:\theta = 0.5$ against alternative hypothesis $H_1:\theta = 0.8$.
\end{example}
note that the likelihood ratio $\lambda(x)=p_1(x)/p_0(x)$ is increasing in the statistic $T(x)$, so the likelihood ratio test rejects $H_0$ for large values of $T(x)$. Since $T(x)\in\{0,1,\dots,5\}$ is discrete, the achievable null rejection probabilities jump: $\Pr_0\{T(X)\ge 5\}=0.03125$ while $\Pr_0\{T(X)\ge 4\}=0.1875$, so one cannot choose a nonrandom cutoff $c$ with $\Pr_0\{T(X)\ge c\}=\alpha$ when, e.g., $\alpha=0.1$. Therefore randomization is needed: define a test function that rejects surely for $T(x)>4$, rejects with probability $0.44$ when $T(x)=4$, and never rejects for $T(x)<4$, yielding $E_0[\phi(X)]=0.1.

The same reasoning explains non-uniqueness: a most powerful test can be defined ``arbitrarily on the set $[\lambda(x)=k]$'' as long as its expectation under $H_0$ equals $\alpha$. Concretely, $\phi$ randomizes uniformly across all $x$ with $T(x)=4$, whereas $\phi^*$ randomizes only on one such point and makes deterministic accept/reject decisions on the other points with $T(x)=4$; both satisfy $E_0[\phi(X)]=E_0[\phi^*(X)]=0.1$ and have equal power $E_1[\phi(X)]=E_1[\phi^*(X)]=0.5079.,

