\begin{remark}\label{con}
It turns out that, in order to obtain the conclusion of Theorem \ref{Theorem1}, we genuinely need \ref{first} to hold for all $k\in \N$. In fact, in Subsection \ref{3.5} we shall present a counterexample for which \ref{first} holds for all $k\ge 1$ with a $k$-independent constant $C>0$ but fails for $k=0$; in this case, the function $\rho \longmapsto \mm(B_\rho(x_0))/\rho^{2C}$
is not non-decreasing.
